e it as three and what we  want to find is how fast is the ladder  moving down the wall so that's this  direction so what we want to do is we  want to find dy DT okay when x equals  fifteen feet okay so now we want to  write an equation that relates all of  these variables okay with what we want  to find and with what we know  the best equation here that relates  these three is the good old Pythagorean  theorem right so we have here is we have  x squared plus y squared equals 17  squared a squared plus b squared equals  c squared right now the next step is we  want to do implicit differentiation okay  with respect to time with respect to T  for time so what we're gonna do is we're  going to take the derivative x squared  which is 2x dx/dt  ok so that's the chain rule plus  derivative of Y squared which is 2y dy  DT we're doing the chain rule because  we're not taking the derivative with  respect to Y we're taking the derivative  with respect to T and then the  derivative of a constant is always going  to be zero okay perfect now let's  substitute in what we know and solve for  what we want to find so we know DX DT is  3 okay we know that X is 15 okay because  they're saying at that moment when X is  15 its 15 feet from the wall plus y is  let's see we're gonna be now let's see  let's draw a diagram here so you can see  it this is 15 this is 17 if we do the  Pythagorean theorem at this particular  point when this is 15 feet Y will be 8  so that's going to be 8 and then dy DT  is what we're solving for equals 0 so we  substitute it everything we know we just  have to solve for dy DT so let's go  ahead and do that this is 30 times 3 is  90 if we subtract it to the other side  we get negative 90 this is 16 if we  divide both sides by 16 we get dy DT  equals negative 19 negative 90 over 16  which reduces down to negative 45 over 8  feet per second now you might notice  this is negative and what that tells us  is that this is going down so the ladder  is sliding like this and it's going down  so that's why the value is negative okay  so that's part a let's look at Part B  now Part B says find the rate at which  the area of the triangle form is  changing when the base is 15 feet from  the wall so they're saying you know  what's the rate that the area of that  triangle is changing  when this is actually like this at  fifteen okay instead of like this okay  so what's the formula for area of this  triangle well area equals base times  height times 1/2 right but in this case  it's going to be one half x times y all  right so you're with me so far so what  we're gonna do is we're going to take  the derivative okay with respect to time  so this is da DT equals one half and I'm  just going to pull the one half out and  we're gonna do the product rule because  x and y are multiplied together so  that's going to be x times three little  Y which is just one dy DT Plus now I'm  going to reverse it I'm going to leave Y  alone I'm going to take the derivative  of X derivative X is one but we have to  do the chain rule  okay so DX DT and then this whole thing  is multiplied by one half that's the  constant multiple rule okay so now all  we have to do is we're solving for da DT  let's substitute in what we know so  we've got 1/2 X we know is fifteen DX DT  is 3 y is we figured out why was eight  and DX DT I'm sorry I made a mistake dy  DT we actually said was negative forty  five over eight and DX DT that's this  direction here is three okay so now if  we multiply all this together we're  going to get the change in the area with  respect to time okay so all you have to  do is multiply all that together now  what you'll notice is this guy actually  comes out to a negative value which  means that the area when you get to this  point here is actually getting smaller  it's decreasing so that's what the  negative tells you if it was positive  then it would be increasing okay Part C  last part on this problem is find the  rate at which the angle now okay between  the ladder and the ground is changing  when the base of the ladder is again  fifteen feet from the wall so they're  saying you know how is this angle right  here theta changing when X is 15 so what  we're trying to solve for in this  problem is D theta DT that's what we  want to find D theta DT when x equals 15  so what's an equation it relates this  angle  and you know in this triangle well the  ladder is always fixed at 17 feet so and  we're interested in we're interested in  this angle here when X is 15 so let's go  ahead and use the cosine we're gonna say  the cosine of theta okay  equals adjacent over hypotenuse so  that's x over 17 all right so now what  we're going to do is we're going to take  the derivative with respect to time so  we're gonna do our implicit  differentiation derivative of cosine is  negative sine theta D theta DT okay and  then this is really--like 117th that's a  constant so it's 117 times the  derivative of X which is 1 DX DT okay  good so now let's substitute in what we  know so the sine of theta okay at this  point in time sine is going to be  opposite over hypotenuse so that's 8 17  times this negative here D theta DT is  what we're solving for the change in the  angle with respect to time and we know  that DX DT is 3 okay so now all we have  to do is multiply by the reciprocal of  negative 8 17 so we're just gonna  multiply by negative 17 8 that way those  cancel negative 17 eighths and if we  simplify this down you can see the 17 s  are cancelling and we get negative 3/8  radians per second so this is calculated  in radians again notice that the angle  is negative which just means that it's  decreasing okay as you're pulling that  ladder away from the wall and that angle  is going to be you know decreasing at  the rate of three eighths of a Radian  per second so I hope this video helped  you understand how to work with related  rates a little bit better especially  this problem about you know the ladder  I'm gonna have a link to another video  another related rate problem it's a cone  type problem so we can dig into that  deeper but subscribe to the channel  check out some of my past videos and I  look forward to helping you in the next  video I'll talk to you soon

